var e={level:`B1`,type:`verb-conjugate`,questions:[{id:`b1-vc-001`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present perfect for life experience: have + never + past participle.`,wrongReasons:{"never been":`You need 'have' before 'never been'.`,"was never":`Experience up to now uses the present perfect: have never been.`,"have never be":`Use the past participle 'been'.`},text:`I ___ (never / be) to Japan.`,answers:[[`have never been`,`'ve never been`]]},{id:`b1-vc-002`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress when another action interrupted.`,wrongReasons:{walked:`'walked' does not show that the action was in progress.`,walking:`You need 'was' before the -ing form.`},text:`While I ___ (walk) home, it started to rain.`,answers:[[`was walking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-003`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'never' for life experience: has + never + past participle. Example: He has never driven a car.`,wrongReasons:{"never used":`You need 'has' before 'never used'.`,"has never use":`After 'has', use the past participle 'used', not the base form.`},text:`My grandmother ___ (never / use) a smartphone.`,answers:[[`has never used`]]},{id:`b1-vc-004`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' for a situation that started in the past and continues now. Example: I have known her since 2015.`,wrongReasons:{know:`With 'since', the present simple is wrong; use 'have known'.`,knew:`The past simple does not connect the past with now; use 'have known'.`},text:`We ___ (know) each other since primary school.`,answers:[[`have known`,`'ve known`]]},{id:`b1-vc-005`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In present perfect questions, use 'have' + subject + 'ever' + past participle. Example: Have you ever tried sushi?`,wrongReasons:{try:`After 'have', the verb must be the past participle 'tried'.`,tryed:`Spelling: the 'y' changes to 'i', so it is 'tried'.`},text:`Have you ever ___ (try) Korean food?`,answers:[[`tried`]]},{id:`b1-vc-006`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'already' for an action that happened earlier than expected and matters now. Example: The film has already started.`,wrongReasons:{"has already leaved":`'Leave' is irregular: the past participle is 'left'.`,"have already left":`'The train' is singular, so use 'has', not 'have'.`},text:`The train ___ (already / leave), so we will take a taxi.`,answers:[[`has already left`]]},{id:`b1-vc-007`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect negative with 'yet' for something that has not happened up to now. Example: She hasn't called yet.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't finish":`With 'yet' and a result that matters now, use the present perfect: haven't finished.`,"not finished":`You need 'have' before 'not finished'.`},text:`I ___ (not / finish) my report yet.`,answers:[[`haven't finished`,`have not finished`,`'ve not finished`]]},{id:`b1-vc-008`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'for' + a period of time that continues up to now. Example: She has lived here for five years.`,wrongReasons:{works:`With 'for ten years' up to now, the present simple is wrong; use 'has worked'.`,"is working":`The present continuous does not show a period from the past to now.`},text:`Tom ___ (work) at the bank for ten years.`,answers:[[`has worked`,`has been working`]]},{id:`b1-vc-009`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'just' for a very recent action. Example: I have just eaten lunch.`,wrongReasons:{"has just sended":`'Send' is irregular: the past participle is 'sent'.`,"have just sent":`The subject 'she' needs 'has', not 'have'.`},text:`She ___ (just / send) me a text message.`,answers:[[`has just sent`,`'s just sent`]]},{id:`b1-vc-010`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'How long' + present perfect to ask about a period up to now. Example: How long have you had that phone?`,wrongReasons:{have:`After the auxiliary 'have', use the past participle 'had'.`,haved:`'Have' is irregular: the past participle is 'had'.`},text:`How long have you ___ (have) this car?`,answers:[[`had`]]},{id:`b1-vc-011`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'so far' to count experiences up to now. Example: I have read five books so far this month.`,wrongReasons:{visited:`'So far' means up to now, so use the present perfect: have visited.`,"has visited":`'They' is plural, so use 'have', not 'has'.`},text:`They ___ (visit) Rome three times so far, and they want to go again.`,answers:[[`have visited`,`'ve visited`]]},{id:`b1-vc-012`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'just' for news that happened a moment ago. Example: The police have just found the car.`,wrongReasons:{"has just discovered":`'The scientists' is plural, so use 'have'.`,"have just discover":`After 'have just', use the past participle 'discovered'.`},text:`The scientists ___ (just / discover) a new kind of frog in the forest.`,answers:[[`have just discovered`]]},{id:`b1-vc-013`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' + a point in time to talk about a period up to now. Example: It hasn't rained since Monday.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't win":`With 'since September' up to now, use the present perfect: hasn't won.`,"hasn't winned":`'Win' is irregular: the past participle is 'won'.`},text:`Our team ___ (not / win) a match since September.`,answers:[[`hasn't won`,`has not won`]]},{id:`b1-vc-014`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'have' in the present perfect, use the past participle. Example: Have you seen my glasses?`,wrongReasons:{saw:`'Saw' is the past simple; after 'have', use the participle 'seen'.`,see:`After 'have', the base form is wrong; use 'seen'.`},text:`Have you ___ (see) my keys? I can't find them.`,answers:[[`seen`]]},{id:`b1-vc-015`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'This is the first time', use the present perfect. Example: It's the first time I have seen snow.`,wrongReasons:{flew:`After 'This is the first time', use the present perfect, not the past simple.`,"have flew":`The past participle of 'fly' is 'flown', not 'flew'.`},text:`This is the first time I ___ (fly) in a helicopter.`,answers:[[`have flown`,`'ve flown`,`have ever flown`,`'ve ever flown`]]},{id:`b1-vc-016`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' + a past event; 'nobody' takes a singular verb. Example: Nobody has called since lunch.`,wrongReasons:{heard:`With 'since she moved', use the present perfect: has heard.`,"have heard":`'Nobody' takes a singular verb, so use 'has'.`},text:`Nobody ___ (hear) from Anna since she moved to Canada.`,answers:[[`has heard`]]},{id:`b1-vc-017`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect of 'be' with 'for' for a state that is still true now. Example: We have been friends for years.`,wrongReasons:{are:`With 'for thirty years' up to now, 'are' is wrong; use 'have been'.`,were:`'Were' means the marriage is over; for a state that continues now, use 'have been'.`},text:`My parents ___ (be) married for thirty years.`,answers:[[`have been`]]},{id:`b1-vc-018`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'since' for a state that began in the past and continues. Example: The road has been closed since Friday.`,wrongReasons:{is:`With 'since the storm', the present simple is wrong; use 'has been'.`,was:`'Was' does not show that the museum is still closed now.`},text:`The museum ___ (be) closed since the storm.`,answers:[[`has been`]]},{id:`b1-vc-019`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Ask about life experience with Have + subject + ever + past participle. Example: Have you ever swum in the sea?`,wrongReasons:{"did you ever ride":`For life experience up to now, use the present perfect: Have you ever ridden...?`,"have you ever rode":`The past participle of 'ride' is 'ridden', not 'rode'.`},text:`___ (you / ever / ride) a horse?`,answers:[[`have you ever ridden`]]},{id:`b1-vc-020`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use hasn't/haven't + past participle with 'yet' for something not done up to now. Example: He hasn't paid yet.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't read":`With 'yet', use the present perfect: hasn't read.`,"hasn't readed":`'Read' is irregular: the past participle is 'read'.`},text:`Kate ___ (not / read) the new rules yet.`,answers:[[`hasn't read`,`has not read`]]},{id:`b1-vc-021`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect (simple or continuous) with 'since' + a year. Example: She has lived in Paris since 2020.`,wrongReasons:{live:`With 'since 2019', the present simple is wrong; use 'have lived'.`,"am living":`The present continuous cannot be used with 'since'; use 'have lived'.`},text:`I ___ (live) in this flat since 2019.`,answers:[[`have lived`,`'ve lived`,`have been living`,`'ve been living`]]},{id:`b1-vc-022`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'already' for something done before now. Example: I have already paid the bill.`,wrongReasons:{"have already book":`After 'have already', use the past participle 'booked'.`,"has already booked":`'We' needs 'have', not 'has'.`},text:`We ___ (already / book) the tickets for the concert.`,answers:[[`have already booked`,`'ve already booked`]]},{id:`b1-vc-023`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect for a change during a period that is not finished. Example: Prices have gone up this month.`,wrongReasons:{"has went":`'Went' is the past simple; the past participle of 'go' is 'gone'.`,goes:`The present simple does not describe a change from the past to now.`},text:`The price of bread ___ (go) up a lot this year, and it is still rising.`,answers:[[`has gone`]]},{id:`b1-vc-024`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In present perfect questions with 'yet', use Has/Have + subject + past participle. Example: Has the bus arrived yet?`,wrongReasons:{call:`After 'has', use the past participle 'called'.`,calling:`The -ing form does not follow 'has' here; use 'called'.`},text:`Has the doctor ___ (call) you back yet?`,answers:[[`called`]]},{id:`b1-vc-025`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`present-perfect`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present perfect with 'so far' for a number of things done up to now. Example: She has painted ten pictures so far.`,wrongReasons:{"has wrote":`'Wrote' is the past simple; the past participle is 'written'.`,wrote:`'So far' means up to now, so use the present perfect: has written.`},text:`He ___ (write) three books so far.`,answers:[[`has written`,`'s written`]]},{id:`b1-vc-026`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a specific time in the past. Example: At six, I was cooking.`,wrongReasons:{watched:`At a specific moment, an action in progress needs the past continuous: were watching.`,"was watching":`'We' is plural, so use 'were', not 'was'.`},text:`At eight o'clock last night, we ___ (watch) a film.`,answers:[[`were watching`]]},{id:`b1-vc-027`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous after 'while' for a longer action that was interrupted. Example: The doorbell rang while I was eating.`,wrongReasons:{cooked:`'Cooked' does not show that the action was in progress.`,"is cooking":`This happened in the past, so use 'was', not 'is'.`},text:`The phone rang while she ___ (cook) dinner.`,answers:[[`was cooking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-028`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the background action and the past simple for the short event. Example: I was walking when I saw him.`,wrongReasons:{drove:`The longer background action needs the past continuous: was driving.`,driving:`You need 'was' before the -ing form.`},text:`I ___ (drive) to work when I saw the accident.`,answers:[[`was driving`]]},{id:`b1-vc-029`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action already in progress when something happened. Example: When I arrived, they were eating.`,wrongReasons:{talked:`'Talked' would mean they started talking after she came in.`,"was talking":`'The students' is plural, so use 'were'.`},text:`When the teacher came in, the students ___ (talk) loudly.`,answers:[[`were talking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-030`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The short action that interrupts a longer one is in the past simple. Example: While I was reading, the lights went out.`,wrongReasons:{"was stealing":`The short, finished action uses the past simple: stole.`,stealed:`'Steal' is irregular: the past simple is 'stole'.`},text:`While Mark was sleeping, someone ___ (steal) his bike.`,answers:[[`stole`]]},{id:`b1-vc-031`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe the weather in the background of a past event. Example: It was raining when I got up.`,wrongReasons:{snowed:`'Snowed' does not describe the situation in progress; use 'was snowing'.`,"is snowing":`This happened this morning, in the past; use 'was'.`},text:`It ___ (snow) when we left the hotel this morning.`,answers:[[`was snowing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-032`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous with 'this time last week' for an action in progress then. Example: This time yesterday, I was flying to Rome.`,wrongReasons:{lay:`'This time last week' needs an action in progress: was lying.`,"was lieing":`Spelling: 'lie' becomes 'lying' in the -ing form.`},text:`This time last week, I ___ (lie) on a beach in Spain.`,answers:[[`was lying`]]},{id:`b1-vc-033`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for the longer action that was interrupted. Example: The baby woke up while we were watching TV.`,wrongReasons:{had:`'Had' does not show that dinner was in progress.`,"was having":`'We' needs 'were', not 'was'.`},text:`The lights went out while we ___ (have) dinner.`,answers:[[`were having`]]},{id:`b1-vc-034`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the activity in progress when an accident happened. Example: He cut his finger while he was cooking.`,wrongReasons:{ran:`The longer activity after 'while' needs the past continuous.`,"was runing":`Spelling: double the 'n' to make 'running'.`},text:`She hurt her knee while she ___ (run) in the park.`,answers:[[`was running`]]},{id:`b1-vc-035`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action that had started before another past event. Example: When I got home, my brother was sleeping.`,wrongReasons:{waited:`'Waited' would mean he started waiting after I arrived.`,"is waiting":`This is a past story, so use 'was', not 'is'.`},text:`When I arrived at the station, my friend ___ (wait) for me.`,answers:[[`was waiting`]]},{id:`b1-vc-036`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The short event that happened during a longer action is in the past simple. Example: While I was shopping, I lost my bag.`,wrongReasons:{"was knocking":`Knocking over a tin is a short, finished action: use 'knocked'.`,knock:`This is the past, so use the past simple 'knocked'.`},text:`While they were painting the kitchen, the dog ___ (knock) over a tin of paint.`,answers:[[`knocked`]]},{id:`b1-vc-037`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action in progress at a past time; 'still' goes after 'was'. Example: At midnight he was still working.`,wrongReasons:{"still cried":`At 3 a.m. the action was in progress, so use the past continuous.`,"is still crying":`This happened in the past; use 'was', not 'is'.`},text:`At 3 a.m. the baby ___ (still / cry).`,answers:[[`was still crying`]]},{id:`b1-vc-038`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to explain what was in progress when something happened. Example: I didn't see you because I was reading.`,wrongReasons:{listened:`'Listened' does not show the activity in progress when you spoke.`,listening:`You need 'was' before the -ing form.`},text:`Sorry, I didn't hear you. I ___ (listen) to music.`,answers:[[`was listening`]]},{id:`b1-vc-039`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for an action already in progress when someone arrived. Example: When we came in, she was singing.`,wrongReasons:{climbed:`'Climbed' would mean he did it after the officer arrived.`,"were climbing":`'A man' is singular, so use 'was'.`},text:`When the police officer arrived, a man ___ (climb) through the window of the shop.`,answers:[[`was climbing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-040`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for the background activity. Example: I found a coin while I was cleaning.`,wrongReasons:{walked:`The background activity after 'while' needs the past continuous.`,"was walking":`'We' needs 'were', not 'was'.`},text:`We met our neighbours while we ___ (walk) the dog.`,answers:[[`were walking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-041`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for the longer action that was interrupted. Example: The power went off while I was typing.`,wrongReasons:{wrote:`'Wrote' does not show that the email was in progress.`,"was writting":`Spelling: 'writing' has only one 't'.`},text:`My computer crashed while I ___ (write) an important email.`,answers:[[`was writing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-042`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In past continuous questions, put 'was/were' before the subject. Example: What were you doing at noon?`,wrongReasons:{"did you talk":`The conversation was in progress when I saw you, so use 'were you talking'.`,"was you talking":`With 'you', use 'were', not 'was'.`},text:`Who ___ (you / talk) to when I saw you outside the bank?`,answers:[[`were you talking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-043`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use while + past continuous for an action in progress when something sudden happened. Example: While we were playing, it started to rain.`,wrongReasons:{danced:`The action was in progress when the music stopped, so use 'were dancing'.`,"was dancing":`'The guests' is plural, so use 'were'.`},text:`While the guests ___ (dance), the music suddenly stopped.`,answers:[[`were dancing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-044`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The short event uses the past simple; the longer action uses the past continuous. Example: She fell over while she was skiing.`,wrongReasons:{"was falling":`Falling asleep is the short event here, so use 'fell'.`,falled:`'Fall' is irregular: the past simple is 'fell'.`},text:`He ___ (fall) asleep while he was reading the newspaper.`,answers:[[`fell`]]},{id:`b1-vc-045`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous to describe a scene in the background of a story. Example: It was getting dark and people were going home.`,wrongReasons:{shone:`To describe the background scene with 'were singing', use 'was shining'.`,"is shining":`This is a past scene, so use 'was'.`},text:`The sun ___ (shine) and the birds were singing when we woke up.`,answers:[[`was shining`]]},{id:`b1-vc-046`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use wasn't/weren't + -ing for an action that was not in progress at a past moment. Example: I wasn't listening when he explained it.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't look":`The situation at that moment needs the past continuous: wasn't looking.`,"weren't looking":`'I' needs 'wasn't', not 'weren't'.`},text:`I ___ (not / look) at the road when the car in front stopped, so I nearly hit it.`,answers:[[`wasn't looking`,`was not looking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-047`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past continuous for what people were doing when an event began. Example: When the fire started, we were eating.`,wrongReasons:{slept:`'Slept' does not show the action in progress when the earthquake started.`,"was sleeping":`'Most people' is plural, so use 'were'.`},text:`When the earthquake started, most people in the city ___ (sleep).`,answers:[[`were sleeping`]]},{id:`b1-vc-048`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`past-continuous`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the past simple for the short event that happens during a longer action. Example: While I was jogging, I met my teacher.`,wrongReasons:{"was seeing":`Seeing someone here is a short event, so use 'saw'.`,seed:`'See' is irregular: the past simple is 'saw'.`},text:`While I was waiting for the bus, I ___ (see) an old school friend.`,answers:[[`saw`]]},{id:`b1-vc-049`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: for facts that are always true, use the present simple in both parts. Example: If you heat ice, it melts.`,wrongReasons:{got:`This is a general fact, so use the present simple, not the past.`,gets:`With 'you', the verb has no -s: 'get'.`},text:`If you mix red and yellow paint, you always ___ (get) orange.`,answers:[[`get`]]},{id:`b1-vc-050`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: for habits, use if + present simple, present simple. Example: If I eat late, I don't feel well.`,wrongReasons:{"won't sleep":`With 'usually' for a habit, use the present simple: don't sleep.`,"doesn't sleep":`With 'I', use 'don't', not 'doesn't'.`},text:`If I drink coffee in the evening, I usually ___ (not / sleep) well.`,answers:[[`don't sleep`,`do not sleep`]]},{id:`b1-vc-051`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Zero conditional for a habit: present simple in both parts. Example: My dog barks if it hears a noise.`,wrongReasons:{hide:`'My cat' is third person singular, so add -s: 'hides'.`,hid:`This is a habit that is true now, so use the present simple.`},text:`My cat always ___ (hide) under the bed if someone rings the doorbell.`,answers:[[`hides`]]},{id:`b1-vc-052`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In the zero conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple. Example: If it gets dark, the lights come on.`,wrongReasons:{"will fall":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'falls'.`,fall:`'The temperature' is singular, so use 'falls'.`},text:`If the temperature ___ (fall) below zero, water turns to ice.`,answers:[[`falls`]]},{id:`b1-vc-053`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Zero conditional with 'when' for a habit: present simple in both parts. Example: When I am tired, I go to bed early.`,wrongReasons:{"will feel":`Do not use 'will' after 'when' in a habit; use 'feels'.`,felt:`'Always goes' shows a present habit, so use the present simple 'feels'.`},text:`When my sister ___ (feel) stressed, she always goes for a long walk.`,answers:[[`feels`]]},{id:`b1-vc-054`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present simple (a fact) or 'will' (a result) in the main clause after if + present. Example: If you press here, the door opens.`,wrongReasons:{stop:`'The machine' is singular, so use 'stops' or 'will stop'.`,stopped:`The if-clause is in the present, so the past 'stopped' does not fit.`},text:`If you press this button, the machine ___ (stop) immediately.`,answers:[[`stops`,`will stop`,`'ll stop`]]},{id:`b1-vc-055`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base verb. Example: If it snows, we will stay at home.`,wrongReasons:{"will rain":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause, even for the future.`,rain:`'It' needs the -s form: 'rains'.`},text:`If it ___ (rain) tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic.`,answers:[[`rains`]]},{id:`b1-vc-056`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: the result clause uses will + base verb. Example: If you practise, you will improve.`,wrongReasons:{pass:`For a future result, use 'will pass'.`,"would pass":`'Would' is for imaginary situations; with 'study' (present), use 'will pass'.`},text:`If you study hard, you ___ (pass) the exam.`,answers:[[`will pass`,`'ll pass`,`are going to pass`,`'re going to pass`]]},{id:`b1-vc-057`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + base verb for the likely future result. Example: You will get wet if you don't take an umbrella.`,wrongReasons:{miss:`This is a future result, so use 'will miss'.`,missed:`The situation is about the future, not the past.`},text:`We ___ (miss) the train if we don't leave now.`,answers:[[`will miss`,`'ll miss`,`are going to miss`,`'re going to miss`]]},{id:`b1-vc-058`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: the if-clause uses the present simple, also in the negative. Example: If he doesn't come, we will start without him.`,wrongReasons:{"won't hurry":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'doesn't hurry'.`,"don't hurry":`With 'she', use 'doesn't', not 'don't'.`},text:`If she ___ (not / hurry), she will be late for the interview.`,answers:[[`doesn't hurry`,`does not hurry`]]},{id:`b1-vc-059`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + base verb, if + present simple. Example: I'll tell you if I find it.`,wrongReasons:{call:`For a promise about the future, use 'will call'.`,"would call":`'Would' is for imaginary situations; after 'if I hear', use 'will call'.`},text:`I ___ (call) you if I hear any news.`,answers:[[`will call`,`'ll call`]]},{id:`b1-vc-060`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In the first conditional, the if-clause uses the present simple of 'be'. Example: If the weather is nice, we'll go out.`,wrongReasons:{"will be":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'is'.`,are:`'The shop' is singular, so use 'is'.`},text:`If the shop ___ (be) open, I'll buy some milk on the way home.`,answers:[[`is`]]},{id:`b1-vc-061`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if not' and is followed by the present simple. Example: Unless you hurry, you'll be late.`,wrongReasons:{"don't wear":`'Unless' already means 'if not', so do not add 'don't'.`,"will wear":`Do not use 'will' after 'unless'.`},text:`Unless you ___ (wear) a coat, you'll catch a cold.`,answers:[[`wear`]]},{id:`b1-vc-062`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple for a possible future event. Example: If they invite me, I will go.`,wrongReasons:{"will offer":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'offer'.`,offers:`'They' is plural, so do not add -s.`},text:`If they ___ (offer) him the job, he will accept it.`,answers:[[`offer`]]},{id:`b1-vc-063`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + base verb for a likely result in the future. Example: The plants will die if you forget them.`,wrongReasons:{lose:`'Soon' shows a future result, so use 'will lose'.`,lost:`This is about the future, so the past 'lost' is wrong.`},text:`The farmers ___ (lose) their crops if it doesn't rain soon.`,answers:[[`will lose`,`are going to lose`]]},{id:`b1-vc-064`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: the negative if-clause uses don't/doesn't + base verb. Example: If you don't eat, you will be hungry.`,wrongReasons:{"won't water":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'don't water'.`,"doesn't water":`With 'you', use 'don't', not 'doesn't'.`},text:`If you ___ (not / water) the tomatoes, they will die.`,answers:[[`don't water`,`do not water`]]},{id:`b1-vc-065`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional question: What will + subject + base verb + if + present simple? Example: What will you say if he asks?`,wrongReasons:{"do you do":`This asks about a possible future situation, so use 'will you do'.`,"would you do":`'Would' is for imaginary situations; with 'is cancelled', use 'will you do'.`},text:`What ___ (you / do) if the flight is cancelled?`,answers:[[`will you do`,`are you going to do`]]},{id:`b1-vc-066`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, even when we talk about the future. Example: If the boss agrees, we will start on Monday.`,wrongReasons:{"will accept":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'accepts'.`,accept:`'The judge' is singular, so add -s: 'accepts'.`},text:`If the judge ___ (accept) the new evidence, the case will continue next week.`,answers:[[`accepts`]]},{id:`b1-vc-067`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + base verb. Example: If I see Tom, I'll ask him.`,wrongReasons:{give:`For a future result, use 'will give'.`,gave:`The situation is in the future, so 'gave' is wrong.`},text:`If I see Maria at the meeting, I ___ (give) her your message.`,answers:[[`will give`,`'ll give`]]},{id:`b1-vc-068`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional negative result: won't + base verb. Example: Your mum won't be happy if you're late.`,wrongReasons:{"isn't":`This is a future result, so use 'won't be'.`,"doesn't be":`We never use 'doesn't' with 'be'; use 'won't be'.`},text:`The doctor ___ (not / be) happy if you don't take your medicine.`,answers:[[`won't be`,`will not be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-069`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: the if-clause uses the present simple. Example: If you drop the glass, it will break.`,wrongReasons:{"will leave":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'leave'.`,left:`The result is in the future, so use the present 'leave', not 'left'.`},text:`If you ___ (leave) the milk outside the fridge, it will go bad.`,answers:[[`leave`]]},{id:`b1-vc-070`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: will + base verb in the main clause. Example: We will open a new shop if business is good.`,wrongReasons:{hires:`The result is in the future, so use 'will hire'.`,hired:`This is about the future, so the past 'hired' is wrong.`},text:`Our company ___ (hire) more staff if sales go up this year.`,answers:[[`will hire`,`is going to hire`]]},{id:`b1-vc-071`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`First conditional: negative if-clause with doesn't/don't + base verb. Example: If she doesn't answer, I'll text her.`,wrongReasons:{"won't come":`Do not use 'will' in the if-clause; use 'doesn't come'.`,"don't come":`'The bus' is singular, so use 'doesn't'.`},text:`If the bus ___ (not / come) in five minutes, we'll walk.`,answers:[[`doesn't come`,`does not come`]]},{id:`b1-vc-072`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive: am/is/are + past participle. Example: Spanish is spoken in Mexico.`,wrongReasons:{speaks:`English does not speak; people speak it, so use the passive.`,"is speaked":`'Speak' is irregular: the past participle is 'spoken'.`},text:`English ___ (speak) in many countries today.`,answers:[[`is spoken`]]},{id:`b1-vc-073`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: was/were + past participle. Example: The castle was built in 1200.`,wrongReasons:{built:`A bridge cannot build itself; use the passive 'was built'.`,"is built":`'In 1885' is in the past, so use 'was', not 'is'.`},text:`This bridge ___ (build) in 1885.`,answers:[[`was built`]]},{id:`b1-vc-074`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for regular actions: are + past participle. Example: The rooms are cleaned every day.`,wrongReasons:{"is delivered":`'The letters' is plural, so use 'are'.`,deliver:`Letters do not deliver; someone delivers them, so use the passive.`},text:`The letters ___ (deliver) every morning at nine.`,answers:[[`are delivered`]]},{id:`b1-vc-075`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive with 'by' for the person who did the action. Example: The window was broken by a ball.`,wrongReasons:{"was catched":`'Catch' is irregular: the past participle is 'caught'.`,caught:`The thief did not catch; the police caught him, so use 'was caught'.`},text:`The thief ___ (catch) by the police last night.`,answers:[[`was caught`]]},{id:`b1-vc-076`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive: is/are + past participle; 'by' shows who does the action. Example: Rice is grown by farmers in Asia.`,wrongReasons:{grows:`With 'by small farmers', the farmers do the action, so use the passive 'is grown'.`,"is growed":`'Grow' is irregular: the past participle is 'grown'.`},text:`A lot of coffee ___ (grow) by small farmers in Colombia.`,answers:[[`is grown`]]},{id:`b1-vc-077`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for a repeated action: are + past participle. Example: The bins are emptied every week.`,wrongReasons:{"is cleaned":`'The windows' is plural, so use 'are'.`,clean:`The windows do not clean anything; use the passive 'are cleaned'.`},text:`The windows ___ (clean) once a month.`,answers:[[`are cleaned`]]},{id:`b1-vc-078`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive for a finished action in the past: was + past participle. Example: This book was written by a doctor.`,wrongReasons:{painted:`The picture did not paint; it was painted, so use the passive.`,"is painted":`Leonardo lived long ago, so use the past 'was painted'.`},text:`The Mona Lisa ___ (paint) by Leonardo da Vinci.`,answers:[[`was painted`]]},{id:`b1-vc-079`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive: are + past participle. Example: These phones are made in Korea.`,wrongReasons:{make:`Cars do not make anything; use the passive 'are made'.`,"are maked":`'Make' is irregular: the past participle is 'made'.`},text:`Today, most of these cars ___ (make) in Japan.`,answers:[[`are made`]]},{id:`b1-vc-080`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive with 'by': was + past participle + by. Example: The school was opened by the queen.`,wrongReasons:{opened:`The hospital did not open itself; the mayor opened it, so use 'was opened'.`,"is opened":`'Last week' is in the past, so use 'was'.`},text:`The new hospital ___ (open) by the mayor last week.`,answers:[[`was opened`]]},{id:`b1-vc-081`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for general facts about how things are produced. Example: Glass is made from sand.`,wrongReasons:{makes:`Paper does not make anything; use the passive 'is made'.`,"is make":`After 'is', use the past participle 'made'.`},text:`Most paper ___ (make) from wood.`,answers:[[`is made`]]},{id:`b1-vc-082`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive with a plural subject: were + past participle. Example: Three houses were damaged in the storm.`,wrongReasons:{"was injured":`'Two people' is plural, so use 'were'.`,injured:`They did not injure anyone; they were injured, so use the passive.`},text:`Two people ___ (injure) in the accident yesterday.`,answers:[[`were injured`]]},{id:`b1-vc-083`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: was + past participle. Example: The game was stopped because of the rain.`,wrongReasons:{cancelled:`The meeting did not cancel anything; use 'was cancelled'.`,"is cancelled":`The reason ('was ill') is in the past, so use 'was'.`},text:`The meeting ___ (cancel) because the manager was ill.`,answers:[[`was cancelled`,`was canceled`]]},{id:`b1-vc-084`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In present passive questions, use Is/Are + subject + past participle. Example: Is the tip included?`,wrongReasons:{include:`After 'Is breakfast', use the past participle 'included'.`,including:`The -ing form makes it active; this question needs the passive 'included'.`},text:`Is breakfast ___ (include) in the price of the room?`,answers:[[`included`]]},{id:`b1-vc-085`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for a regular action: is + past participle. Example: The post is collected every day.`,wrongReasons:{checks:`The homework does not check; the teacher checks it, so use 'is checked'.`,"are checked":`'Homework' is uncountable and singular, so use 'is'.`},text:`Our homework ___ (check) by the teacher every Friday.`,answers:[[`is checked`]]},{id:`b1-vc-086`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Negative past passive: wasn't/weren't + past participle. Example: The car wasn't repaired last week.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't make":`The film did not make anything; use the passive 'wasn't made'.`,"wasn't maked":`'Make' is irregular: the past participle is 'made'.`},text:`The film ___ (not / make) in Hollywood; it was filmed in Ireland.`,answers:[[`wasn't made`,`was not made`]]},{id:`b1-vc-087`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past passive question: (Wh-) + was/were + subject + past participle. Example: When was this house built?`,wrongReasons:{"did the telephone invent":`The telephone did not invent anything; use 'was the telephone invented'.`,"was the telephone invent":`After 'was', use the past participle 'invented'.`},text:`When ___ (the telephone / invent)?`,answers:[[`was the telephone invented`]]},{id:`b1-vc-088`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for a regular action with a plural subject. Example: Many letters are sent every day.`,wrongReasons:{"is cut":`'Thousands of trees' is plural, so use 'are'.`,cut:`Trees do not cut anything; use the passive 'are cut'.`},text:`Thousands of trees ___ (cut) down here every year.`,answers:[[`are cut`]]},{id:`b1-vc-089`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive for a routine: is + past participle. Example: The grass is cut on Saturdays.`,wrongReasons:{collects:`Rubbish does not collect anything; use 'is collected'.`,"are collected":`'Rubbish' is uncountable, so use 'is'.`},text:`In our street, the rubbish ___ (collect) on Mondays.`,answers:[[`is collected`]]},{id:`b1-vc-090`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive with a plural subject: were + past participle. Example: The animals were saved in 2010.`,wrongReasons:{freed:`The prisoners did not free anyone; they were freed, so use the passive.`,"was freed":`'The prisoners' is plural, so use 'were'.`},text:`The prisoners ___ (free) in 2015 after ten years in jail.`,answers:[[`were freed`]]},{id:`b1-vc-091`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: was + past participle (+ by). Example: This letter was written by my aunt.`,wrongReasons:{"was wrote":`'Wrote' is the past simple; the past participle is 'written'.`,wrote:`The song did not write; it was written, so use the passive.`},text:`This song ___ (write) by my grandfather in 1960.`,answers:[[`was written`]]},{id:`b1-vc-092`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Past simple passive: the verb agrees with the plural subject 'results'. Example: The winners were named last night.`,wrongReasons:{"was announced":`The subject is 'the results' (plural), so use 'were'.`,announced:`The results did not announce; they were announced, so use the passive.`},text:`The results of the election ___ (announce) yesterday evening.`,answers:[[`were announced`]]},{id:`b1-vc-093`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Negative present passive: am/is/are + not + past participle. Example: Dogs aren't allowed in the shop.`,wrongReasons:{"don't allow":`Visitors do not allow; they are not allowed, so use the passive.`,"are not allow":`After 'are not', use the past participle 'allowed'.`},text:`Visitors ___ (not / allow) to take photos inside the museum.`,answers:[[`aren't allowed`,`are not allowed`]]},{id:`b1-vc-094`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Present simple passive: are + past participle for a plural subject. Example: Cash is accepted here.`,wrongReasons:{accept:`Credit cards do not accept anything; use the passive 'are accepted'.`,"is accepted":`'Credit cards' is plural, so use 'are'.`},text:`Credit cards ___ (accept) in all our shops.`,answers:[[`are accepted`]]},{id:`b1-vc-095`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form describes a past habit or state that is not true now. Example: She used to live in Rome.`,wrongReasons:{"use to work":`In a positive sentence, write 'used to' with -d.`,"used to working":`'Used to' for past habits is followed by the base form: used to work.`},text:`My grandfather ___ (used to / work) in a coal mine, but now he grows vegetables.`,answers:[[`used to work`]]},{id:`b1-vc-096`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Enjoy' is followed by the -ing form. Example: She enjoys reading.`,wrongReasons:{"to cook":`'Enjoy' is never followed by 'to'; use -ing.`,cook:`After 'enjoy', the verb takes -ing: cooking.`},text:`My brother enjoys ___ (cook) for the whole family at weekends.`,answers:[[`cooking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-097`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' for a prediction based on what we can see now. Example: Look, he's going to fall!`,wrongReasons:{"are going to fall":`'Glass' is singular, so use 'is going to'.`,"is going to falling":`After 'going to', use the base form: fall.`},text:`Be careful! That glass ___ (be going to / fall) off the table.`,answers:[[`is going to fall`]]},{id:`b1-vc-098`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'shouldn't' + base form to give advice not to do something. Example: You shouldn't stay up so late.`,wrongReasons:{"shouldn't to eat":`After 'shouldn't', use the base form without 'to'.`,"don't should eat":`Modals make the negative with 'not' after them: shouldn't eat.`},text:`You ___ (should / not / eat) so much fast food; it's bad for your health.`,answers:[[`shouldn't eat`,`should not eat`]]},{id:`b1-vc-099`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The negative of 'used to' is didn't + use to + base form. Example: I didn't use to like tea.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't used to have":`After 'didn't', write 'use to' without -d.`,"not used to have":`You need 'didn't' to make the negative of 'used to'.`},text:`We ___ (not / use to / have) a car when I was little, so we walked everywhere.`,answers:[[`didn't use to have`,`did not use to have`]]},{id:`b1-vc-100`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Decide' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: He decided to stay.`,wrongReasons:{taking:`'Decide' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,take:`You need 'to' before the verb after 'decide'.`},text:`We decided ___ (take) the train because the roads were busy.`,answers:[[`to take`]]},{id:`b1-vc-101`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for a decision made at the moment of speaking. Example: I'm hungry. I'll make a sandwich.`,wrongReasons:{answer:`This is a sudden decision about the future, so use 'will': I'll answer.`,"am going to answer":`The decision is made right now, so use 'will', not 'going to'.`},text:`The phone is ringing. Don't get up, I ___ (answer) it.`,answers:[[`'ll answer`,`will answer`]]},{id:`b1-vc-102`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Have to' + base form shows a rule or obligation. Example: Drivers have to stop at red lights.`,wrongReasons:{"has to wear":`'Students' is plural, so use 'have to'.`,"have to wearing":`After 'have to', use the base form: wear.`},text:`In this school, all students ___ (have to / wear) a uniform.`,answers:[[`have to wear`]]},{id:`b1-vc-103`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Questions with 'used to' are made with did + subject + use to + base form. Example: Did you use to walk to school?`,wrongReasons:{"did you used to go":`After 'did', use 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"you used to go":`A question needs 'did' before the subject.`},text:`Where ___ (you / use to / go) on holiday when you were a child?`,answers:[[`did you use to go`]]},{id:`b1-vc-104`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Would you mind' is followed by the -ing form. Example: Would you mind waiting a minute?`,wrongReasons:{"to close":`'Mind' takes the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,close:`After 'mind', add -ing: closing.`},text:`Would you mind ___ (close) the window? It's cold in here.`,answers:[[`closing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-105`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' / 'won't' for promises. Example: I won't be late, I promise.`,wrongReasons:{"don't tell":`A promise about the future uses 'will': I won't tell.`,"won't to tell":`After 'won't', use the base form without 'to'.`},text:`I promise I ___ (not / tell) anyone your secret.`,answers:[[`won't tell`,`will not tell`]]},{id:`b1-vc-106`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The past of 'must' / 'have to' is 'had to'. Example: I had to leave early last night.`,wrongReasons:{"must work":`'Must' has no past form; use 'had to' for yesterday.`,"have to work":`The action was yesterday, so use the past: had to.`},text:`My dad ___ (have to / work) late yesterday, so he missed dinner.`,answers:[[`had to work`]]},{id:`b1-vc-107`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Used to be' describes a past state that has changed. Example: This park used to be a farm.`,wrongReasons:{"was used to be":`Do not add 'was'; 'used to be' is already a past form.`,"use to be":`In a positive sentence the form is 'used to', with -d.`},text:`This building ___ (used to / be) a cinema; now it is a supermarket.`,answers:[[`used to be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-108`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After a preposition like 'at', 'in' or 'of', use the -ing form. Example: He is good at singing.`,wrongReasons:{"to fix":`After the preposition 'at', use -ing, not 'to' + verb.`,fix:`A verb after a preposition needs -ing: fixing.`},text:`She is very good at ___ (fix) old bicycles.`,answers:[[`fixing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-109`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous or 'going to' for future plans that are already arranged. Example: We're having dinner with Sam on Friday.`,wrongReasons:{"will have":`The party is already arranged, so use the present continuous or 'going to', not 'will'.`,have:`The present simple does not show a future plan here.`},text:`We ___ (have) a party next Saturday. We have already invited twenty people.`,answers:[[`are having`,`'re having`,`are going to have`,`'re going to have`]]},{id:`b1-vc-110`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Don't have to' means it is not necessary. Example: You don't have to bring food; we have plenty.`,wrongReasons:{"mustn't pay":`'Mustn't' means it is forbidden; here it is just not necessary.`,"doesn't have to pay":`'You' takes 'don't', not 'doesn't'.`},text:`You ___ (not / have to / pay) for the museum on Sundays; it's free.`,answers:[[`don't have to pay`,`do not have to pay`]]},{id:`b1-vc-111`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base form for things people often did in the past. Example: People used to send postcards.`,wrongReasons:{"used to wrote":`After 'used to', use the base form 'write', not the past form.`,"were used to write":`'Be used to' means 'be familiar with' and takes -ing; for a past habit say 'used to write'.`},text:`Before the internet, people ___ (used to / write) letters to their friends much more often.`,answers:[[`used to write`]]},{id:`b1-vc-112`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Promise' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: They promised to call.`,wrongReasons:{showing:`'Promise' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,"to showing":`After 'to' here, use the base form: to show.`},text:`The museum guide promised ___ (show) us the oldest paintings after lunch.`,answers:[[`to show`]]},{id:`b1-vc-113`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'as soon as', 'when' and 'before', use the present simple to talk about the future. Example: I'll text you when I arrive.`,wrongReasons:{"will get":`After 'as soon as' we use the present simple, not 'will'.`,got:`Use the present, not the past, for a future time after 'as soon as'.`},text:`Call me as soon as you ___ (get) to the hotel.`,answers:[[`get`]]},{id:`b1-vc-114`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Mustn't' + base form means something is not allowed. Example: You mustn't park here.`,wrongReasons:{"don't have to use":`'Don't have to' means it is not necessary; here it is forbidden.`,"mustn't to use":`After 'mustn't', use the base form without 'to'.`},text:`You ___ (must / not / use) your phone during the exam.`,answers:[[`mustn't use`,`must not use`]]},{id:`b1-vc-115`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Didn't use to + base form shows something that was not true in the past but is true now. Example: He didn't use to drive.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't used to like":`With 'didn't', the verb is 'use to', without -d.`,"doesn't use to like":`'Used to' only talks about the past; it has no present form.`},text:`My sister ___ (not / use to / like) vegetables, but now she eats them every day.`,answers:[[`didn't use to like`,`did not use to like`]]},{id:`b1-vc-116`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Think of' / 'think about' + -ing talks about a possible plan. Example: We're thinking of moving.`,wrongReasons:{"to join":`'Of' is a preposition, so the verb after it takes -ing.`,join:`Add -ing after the preposition 'of': joining.`},text:`I'm thinking of ___ (join) a photography club.`,answers:[[`joining`]]},{id:`b1-vc-117`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' after 'I think' for predictions and opinions about the future. Example: I think it will be sunny tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{do:`This is a prediction about the future, so use 'will do'.`,"will doing":`After 'will', use the base form: will do.`},text:`I think robots ___ (do) most household jobs in the future.`,answers:[[`will do`,`'ll do`,`are going to do`]]},{id:`b1-vc-118`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Might' + base form shows that something is possible. Example: She might call tonight.`,wrongReasons:{"might rains":`After a modal, do not add -s: might rain.`,"might to rain":`After 'might', use the base form without 'to'.`},text:`Take an umbrella. It ___ (might / rain) later.`,answers:[[`might rain`]]},{id:`b1-vc-119`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Make 'used to' questions with did + subject + use to. Example: Did your brother use to play chess?`,wrongReasons:{"used your teacher to give":`Modern English makes these questions with 'did': Did your teacher use to give...?`,"did your teacher used to give":`After 'did', drop the -d: use to give.`},text:`___ (your teacher / use to / give) you a lot of homework?`,answers:[[`Did your teacher use to give`]]},{id:`b1-vc-120`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Hope' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: I hope to see you soon.`,wrongReasons:{finishing:`'Hope' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,finish:`You need 'to' before the verb after 'hope'.`},text:`They hope ___ (finish) the new bridge by next summer.`,answers:[[`to finish`]]},{id:`b1-vc-121`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'be going to' + base form for intentions and plans. Example: I'm going to learn to drive.`,wrongReasons:{"are going to study":`'My sister' is singular, so use 'is going to'.`,"is going to studying":`After 'going to', use the base form: study.`},text:`My sister ___ (be going to / study) medicine at university next year.`,answers:[[`is going to study`]]},{id:`b1-vc-122`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The past of 'can' for ability is 'could'. Example: When she was young, she could run very fast.`,wrongReasons:{"can swim":`This is about the past, so use 'could'.`,"could swam":`After 'could', use the base form: swim.`},text:`When I was ten, I ___ (can / swim) across the lake.`,answers:[[`could swim`]]},{id:`b1-vc-123`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'There used to be' says that something existed in the past but not now. Example: There used to be a school here.`,wrongReasons:{"used to been":`After 'used to', use the base form 'be', not 'been'.`,"use to be":`Positive sentences need 'used', with -d.`},text:`There ___ (used to / be) a small bakery on this corner, but it closed last year.`,answers:[[`used to be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-124`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'thank you for', use the -ing form. Example: Thank you for coming.`,wrongReasons:{"to help":`'For' is a preposition here, so use -ing: helping.`,help:`The verb after 'for' needs -ing.`},text:`Thank you for ___ (help) me with my project.`,answers:[[`helping`]]},{id:`b1-vc-125`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' for offers and decisions made at the moment of speaking. Example: You look cold. I'll get you a jacket.`,wrongReasons:{lend:`This is an offer for the future, so use 'will': I'll lend.`,"am going to lend":`The offer is decided right now, so use 'will', not 'going to'.`},text:`"I've lost my pen." "No problem, I ___ (lend) you mine."`,answers:[[`'ll lend`,`will lend`]]},{id:`b1-vc-126`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'couldn't' + base form for something you were not able to do in the past. Example: I couldn't find my keys this morning.`,wrongReasons:{"can't come":`The party was last Saturday, so use the past: couldn't.`,"couldn't came":`After 'couldn't', use the base form: come.`},text:`I'm sorry, I ___ (can / not / come) to your party last Saturday because I was ill.`,answers:[[`couldn't come`,`could not come`]]},{id:`b1-vc-127`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Be used to' + -ing means something is normal or familiar for you. Example: I'm used to living alone.`,wrongReasons:{"am used to work":`After 'be used to', use the -ing form: working.`,"used to work":`'Used to work' is a past habit; here you mean night work is normal for you now.`},text:`I'm a nurse, so I ___ (be used to / work) at night. It doesn't feel strange any more.`,answers:[[`am used to working`,`'m used to working`]]},{id:`b1-vc-128`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Agree' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: She agreed to help us.`,wrongReasons:{lending:`'Agree' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,lend:`Put 'to' before the verb after 'agree'.`},text:`My parents agreed ___ (lend) me some money for the course.`,answers:[[`to lend`]]},{id:`b1-vc-129`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Ask about someone's plans with the present continuous or 'going to'. Example: What are you doing at the weekend?`,wrongReasons:{"do you do":`'Do you do' asks about habits; for plans use 'are you doing'.`,"you are doing":`In a question, put 'are' before 'you'.`},text:`What ___ (you / do) this evening? Do you want to come to the cinema with us?`,answers:[[`are you doing`,`are you going to do`]]},{id:`b1-vc-130`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`With he/she/it, 'have to' becomes 'has to'. Example: He has to take the bus to work.`,wrongReasons:{"have to get":`'Sara' is third person singular, so use 'has to'.`,"must to get":`'Must' is never followed by 'to'; here use 'has to get'.`},text:`Sara ___ (have to / get) up at six every morning for her new job.`,answers:[[`has to get`]]},{id:`b1-vc-131`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Get used to' + -ing means slowly become familiar with something. Example: You will get used to waking up early.`,wrongReasons:{"get used to drive":`In 'get used to', 'to' is a preposition, so use -ing: driving.`,"get use to driving":`Write 'used' with -d in 'get used to'.`},text:`When she moved to Canada, it took her months to ___ (get used to / drive) on icy roads.`,answers:[[`get used to driving`]]},{id:`b1-vc-132`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Give up' (= stop doing) is followed by the -ing form. Example: He gave up smoking.`,wrongReasons:{"to eat":`Phrasal verbs like 'give up' are followed by -ing, not 'to'.`,eat:`After 'give up', add -ing: eating.`},text:`Last year my father gave up ___ (eat) sweets.`,answers:[[`eating`]]},{id:`b1-vc-133`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In the first conditional, use if + present simple, then 'will' + base form. Example: If it rains, we'll stay at home.`,wrongReasons:{walk:`The result of a real future condition uses 'will': we'll walk.`,"would walk":`'Would' is for unreal situations; this is a real possibility, so use 'will'.`},text:`If the weather is nice tomorrow, we ___ (walk) to the beach.`,answers:[[`will walk`,`'ll walk`,`are going to walk`,`'re going to walk`]]},{id:`b1-vc-134`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Questions with 'have to' use do/does + subject + have to. Example: Does she have to work on Sunday?`,wrongReasons:{"has jake to take":`Make questions with 'does': Does Jake have to take...?`,"does jake has to take":`After 'does', use 'have', not 'has'.`},text:`___ (Jake / have to / take) the test again next week?`,answers:[[`Does Jake have to take`]]},{id:`b1-vc-135`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base form for a repeated action in the past. Example: We used to swim in the river.`,wrongReasons:{"used to playing":`Without 'be' or 'get', 'used to' takes the base form: used to play.`,"use to play":`The positive form needs -d: used to play.`},text:`The children ___ (used to / play) in the street because there were not many cars.`,answers:[[`used to play`]]},{id:`b1-vc-136`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Refuse' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: The child refused to sleep.`,wrongReasons:{answering:`'Refuse' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,answer:`You need 'to' before the verb after 'refuse'.`},text:`The manager refused ___ (answer) the journalist's questions.`,answers:[[`to answer`]]},{id:`b1-vc-137`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'won't be' (or 'am not going to be') for a future state. Example: I won't be at home tonight.`,wrongReasons:{"am not be":`You cannot put 'am' before 'be'; use 'won't be'.`,"don't be":`'Don't be' is an order; for the future use 'won't be'.`},text:`I ___ (not / be) at work tomorrow, so please send me a text instead of an email.`,answers:[[`won't be`,`will not be`,`am not going to be`,`'m not going to be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-138`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Can' has no future form, so use 'will be able to' + base form. Example: You'll be able to drive after the course.`,wrongReasons:{"will can walk":`'Can' cannot follow 'will'; use 'will be able to'.`,"will be able walk":`You need 'to' after 'able': able to walk.`},text:`After the operation, my grandmother ___ (be able to / walk) without help in a few weeks.`,answers:[[`will be able to walk`,`'ll be able to walk`]]},{id:`b1-vc-139`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'did' in a question, use 'use to' + base form. Example: Did she use to work in a bank?`,wrongReasons:{"used to live":`'Did' already shows the past, so write 'use to', not 'used to'.`,"use to living":`After 'use to' in this question, use the base form 'live'.`},text:`Did your parents ___ (use to / live) in the countryside?`,answers:[[`use to live`]]},{id:`b1-vc-140`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Imagine' is followed by the -ing form. Example: Can you imagine flying to the moon?`,wrongReasons:{"to live":`'Imagine' takes the -ing form, not 'to' + verb.`,live:`After 'imagine', add -ing: living.`},text:`I can't imagine ___ (live) without my phone.`,answers:[[`living`]]},{id:`b1-vc-141`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' or 'going to' for a future result. Example: Run or you'll miss the bus!`,wrongReasons:{miss:`This is a future result, so you need 'will' or 'going to'.`,missed:`The beginning has not happened yet, so do not use the past.`},text:`The film starts at eight. Hurry up or we ___ (miss) the beginning!`,answers:[[`'ll miss`,`will miss`,`are going to miss`,`'re going to miss`]]},{id:`b1-vc-142`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Should' + base form gives advice. Example: You should see a doctor.`,wrongReasons:{"should to go":`After 'should', use the base form without 'to'.`,"should going":`A modal is followed by the base form, not -ing.`},text:`You look tired. You ___ (should / go) to bed early tonight.`,answers:[[`should go`]]},{id:`b1-vc-143`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Never used to' + base form is another way to make a negative past habit. Example: I never used to read the news.`,wrongReasons:{"never use to eat":`Without 'did', the form is 'used to', with -d.`,"didn't never use to eat":`Do not use 'didn't' and 'never' together.`},text:`He ___ (never / use to / eat) breakfast, but now he never misses it.`,answers:[[`never used to eat`]]},{id:`b1-vc-144`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Forget to' + base form is about something you must do. Example: Don't forget to buy milk.`,wrongReasons:{watering:`'Forget + -ing' is about memories; for a task you must do, use 'forget to'.`,water:`You need 'to' before the verb: to water.`},text:`Don't forget ___ (water) the plants while I'm away.`,answers:[[`to water`]]},{id:`b1-vc-145`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'before', 'when' and 'after', use the present simple for the future. Example: Finish your homework before you go out.`,wrongReasons:{"will come":`After 'before' we use the present simple, not 'will'.`,came:`This is about the future, so do not use the past simple.`},text:`I'll make dinner before you ___ (come) home.`,answers:[[`come`]]},{id:`b1-vc-146`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Might not' + base form shows a negative possibility. Example: He might not come to the meeting.`,wrongReasons:{"might don't have":`Do not use 'don't' after a modal; say 'might not have'.`,"don't might have":`Put 'not' after the modal: might not have.`},text:`We ___ (might / not / have) time to visit the castle, because the tour is very short.`,answers:[[`might not have`,`mightn't have`]]},{id:`b1-vc-147`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form contrasts the past with the present. Example: They used to keep sheep, but now they keep goats.`,wrongReasons:{"used to grew":`After 'used to', write the base form 'grow'.`,"use to grow":`Add -d in a positive sentence: used to grow.`},text:`Farmers in this valley ___ (used to / grow) rice, but now they grow fruit.`,answers:[[`used to grow`]]},{id:`b1-vc-148`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`In 'look forward to', 'to' is a preposition, so use the -ing form. Example: I'm looking forward to meeting you.`,wrongReasons:{see:`'To' in 'look forward to' is a preposition, so the verb needs -ing.`,saw:`Do not use the past form; after 'look forward to' use -ing.`},text:`I'm looking forward to ___ (see) my cousins next month.`,answers:[[`seeing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-149`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'going to' (or the present continuous) for a plan that someone has already prepared. Example: She has bought flour; she's going to bake a cake.`,wrongReasons:{"will paint":`Mark has already prepared, so this is a plan: use 'going to'.`,paints:`The present simple does not show a plan for this weekend.`},text:`Mark has bought paint and brushes. He ___ (paint) his bedroom this weekend.`,answers:[[`is going to paint`,`'s going to paint`,`is painting`,`'s painting`]]},{id:`b1-vc-150`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Must' + base form shows a strong rule. Example: Passengers must show their tickets.`,wrongReasons:{"must to leave":`'Must' is followed by the base form without 'to'.`,"must leaving":`After 'must', do not use -ing; use the base form.`},text:`The sign says visitors ___ (must / leave) their bags at the entrance.`,answers:[[`must leave`]]},{id:`b1-vc-151`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Not be used to' + -ing means something is not familiar for you. Example: She wasn't used to the cold weather.`,wrongReasons:{"wasn't used to speak":`After 'be used to', use the -ing form: speaking.`,"didn't use to speak":`That describes a past habit; here you mean speaking to groups was not familiar to you.`},text:`I ___ (not / be used to / speak) in front of large groups, so I was nervous at the conference.`,answers:[[`wasn't used to speaking`,`was not used to speaking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-152`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Stop' + -ing means you no longer do something. Example: She stopped eating meat.`,wrongReasons:{"to drink":`'Stop to drink' means stop in order to drink; here he should quit, so use -ing.`,drink:`After 'stop', add -ing: drinking.`},text:`The doctor advised him to stop ___ (drink) coffee in the evening.`,answers:[[`drinking`]]},{id:`b1-vc-153`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'I'm sure', use 'will' to make a prediction. Example: I'm sure she'll love the present.`,wrongReasons:{pass:`This is a prediction about the future, so use 'will pass'.`,passed:`The exam is in the future, so do not use the past.`},text:`Don't worry about the exam. I'm sure you ___ (pass).`,answers:[[`'ll pass`,`will pass`,`are going to pass`,`'re going to pass`]]},{id:`b1-vc-154`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`To ask for help, use Can + subject + base form. Example: Can you pass me the salt?`,wrongReasons:{"you can help":`In a question, put 'can' before the subject.`,"can you to help":`After 'can', use the base form without 'to'.`},text:`___ (you / can / help) me carry these chairs, please?`,answers:[[`Can you help`]]},{id:`b1-vc-155`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base form for habits that were common in the past. Example: People used to rent films from shops.`,wrongReasons:{"used to buying":`'Used to' for past habits needs the base form: used to buy.`,"were used to buy":`Do not add 'were'; a past habit is just 'used to buy'.`},text:`In the 1990s, most people ___ (used to / buy) music on CDs.`,answers:[[`used to buy`]]},{id:`b1-vc-156`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Offer' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: He offered to drive us home.`,wrongReasons:{feeding:`'Offer' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,feed:`You need 'to' before the verb after 'offer'.`},text:`Our neighbours offered ___ (feed) the cat while we were on holiday.`,answers:[[`to feed`]]},{id:`b1-vc-157`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' to offer help at the moment of speaking. Example: Wait, I'll open the door for you.`,wrongReasons:{carry:`An offer for the future needs 'will': I'll carry.`,"am carrying":`The present continuous is for plans made earlier; this is a quick offer, so use 'will'.`},text:`These bags are really heavy. Give me one; I ___ (carry) it for you.`,answers:[[`'ll carry`,`will carry`]]},{id:`b1-vc-158`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'May' + base form shows that something is possible now. Example: He may be at home.`,wrongReasons:{maybe:`'Maybe' is an adverb; the verb form is two words: may be.`,"may is":`After 'may', use the base form 'be'.`},text:`I'm not sure where Anna is. She ___ (may / be) at the library.`,answers:[[`may be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-159`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form shows a habit that has stopped. Example: My mother used to bite her nails.`,wrongReasons:{"used to smoking":`Without 'be', 'used to' takes the base form: used to smoke.`,"use to smoke":`In positive sentences, 'used' needs -d.`},text:`My uncle ___ (used to / smoke), but he stopped ten years ago.`,answers:[[`used to smoke`]]},{id:`b1-vc-160`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Practise' is followed by the -ing form. Example: He practises speaking French.`,wrongReasons:{"to play":`'Practise' takes -ing, not 'to' + verb.`,play:`After 'practise', add -ing: playing.`},text:`She practises ___ (play) the violin for an hour every day.`,answers:[[`playing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-161`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will be' to talk about a future state or time. Example: Dinner will be ready soon.`,wrongReasons:{am:`'In five minutes' is the future, so use 'will be'.`,"am being":`'Be' is not normally used in the continuous here; say 'I'll be ready'.`},text:`Wait for me. I ___ (be) ready in five minutes.`,answers:[[`'ll be`,`will be`,`am going to be`,`'m going to be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-162`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`With he/she/it, the negative is 'doesn't have to' (= not necessary). Example: She doesn't have to work on Fridays.`,wrongReasons:{"don't have to wear":`'He' takes 'doesn't', not 'don't'.`,"mustn't wear":`'Mustn't' means it is forbidden; he can wear a suit, it is just not necessary.`},text:`He ___ (not / have to / wear) a suit at work, so he usually wears jeans.`,answers:[[`doesn't have to wear`,`does not have to wear`]]},{id:`b1-vc-163`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Will get used to' + -ing says you will become familiar with something. Example: You'll get used to driving on the left.`,wrongReasons:{"will soon get used to use":`In 'get used to', 'to' is a preposition, so the verb takes -ing: using.`,"soon get used to use":`You need 'will' for the future and -ing after 'get used to'.`},text:`Don't worry about the new software. You ___ (soon / get used to / use) it.`,answers:[[`will soon get used to using`,`'ll soon get used to using`]]},{id:`b1-vc-164`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Afford' is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: I can't afford to travel this summer.`,wrongReasons:{buying:`'Afford' takes 'to' + base form, not -ing.`,buy:`You need 'to' before the verb after 'afford'.`},text:`We can't afford ___ (buy) a new car this year.`,answers:[[`to buy`]]},{id:`b1-vc-165`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'Shall I...?' + base form to offer help. Example: Shall I make some tea?`,wrongReasons:{"will i carry":`To offer help, use 'Shall I...?', not 'Will I...?'.`,"shall i to carry":`After 'shall', use the base form without 'to'.`},text:`___ (shall / I / carry) this box upstairs for you?`,answers:[[`Shall I carry`]]},{id:`b1-vc-166`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'had to' + base form for an obligation in the past. Example: They had to walk home.`,wrongReasons:{"must wait":`'Must' has no past form; use 'had to'.`,"have to waited":`Use 'had to' for the past, then the base form: wait.`},text:`We ___ (have to / wait) for over an hour at the airport last night.`,answers:[[`had to wait`]]},{id:`b1-vc-167`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Questions with 'used to' follow this order: question word + did + subject + use to + base form. Example: What did you use to eat?`,wrongReasons:{"people used to travel":`This is a question, so you need 'did' before the subject.`,"did people used to travel":`After 'did', write 'use to' without -d.`},text:`How ___ (people / use to / travel) before there were trains?`,answers:[[`did people use to travel`]]},{id:`b1-vc-168`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Finish' is followed by the -ing form. Example: I finished cleaning the kitchen.`,wrongReasons:{"to write":`'Finish' takes -ing, not 'to' + verb.`,write:`After 'finish', add -ing: writing.`},text:`Have you finished ___ (write) your report yet?`,answers:[[`writing`]]},{id:`b1-vc-169`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The negative of 'be going to' is am/is/are + not + going to + base form. Example: We aren't going to move.`,wrongReasons:{"isn't going to sell":`'They' is plural, so use 'aren't going to'.`,"aren't going to selling":`After 'going to', use the base form: sell.`},text:`They ___ (not / be going to / sell) their house after all; they have decided to stay.`,answers:[[`aren't going to sell`,`are not going to sell`,`'re not going to sell`]]},{id:`b1-vc-170`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Should' + base form gives advice or a recommendation. Example: Children should eat fruit every day.`,wrongReasons:{"should drinking":`After 'should', use the base form, not -ing.`,"should drank":`A modal is followed by the base form, not the past.`},text:`Doctors say we ___ (should / drink) at least two litres of water a day.`,answers:[[`should drink`]]},{id:`b1-vc-171`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`For a past habit that was not true, use didn't use to + base form. Example: The shop didn't use to sell bread.`,wrongReasons:{"wasn't used to open":`'Be used to' means 'be familiar with'; for a past habit use 'didn't use to'.`,"didn't used to open":`After 'didn't', the correct form is 'use to'.`},text:`Our office ___ (not / use to / open) on Saturdays, but now it does.`,answers:[[`didn't use to open`,`did not use to open`]]},{id:`b1-vc-172`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Be tired of' is followed by the -ing form. Example: I'm tired of hearing the same song.`,wrongReasons:{"to wait":`'Of' is a preposition, so use -ing after it.`,wait:`The verb after 'tired of' needs -ing: waiting.`},text:`The students were tired of ___ (wait) for the bus in the rain.`,answers:[[`waiting`]]},{id:`b1-vc-173`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After a condition with 'unless' (= if not) + present, use 'will' for the result. Example: Unless you hurry, you'll miss the train.`,wrongReasons:{are:`The result is in the future, so use 'will be'.`,"would be":`This is a real possibility, so use 'will', not 'would'.`},text:`Unless we leave now, we ___ (be) late for the concert.`,answers:[[`'ll be`,`will be`,`are going to be`,`'re going to be`]]},{id:`b1-vc-174`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Must' + base form shows that something is necessary. Example: You must wear gloves in the lab.`,wrongReasons:{"must to read":`There is no 'to' after 'must'.`,"must reads":`After a modal, never add -s: must read.`},text:`Before you start the experiment, you ___ (must / read) the safety rules.`,answers:[[`must read`]]},{id:`b1-vc-175`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Is used to' + -ing shows that something is normal for someone now. Example: She is used to working long hours.`,wrongReasons:{"is used to get":`After 'be used to', the verb takes -ing: getting.`,"used to get":`'Used to get' is a past habit; Tom still gets up early, so use 'is used to getting'.`},text:`Tom is a farmer, so he ___ (be used to / get) up at five o'clock.`,answers:[[`is used to getting`]]},{id:`b1-vc-176`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Need' with a person as subject is followed by 'to' + base form. Example: You need to rest.`,wrongReasons:{finding:`When a person needs to do something, use 'need to' + base form.`,find:`You need 'to' before the verb after 'need'.`},text:`I really need ___ (find) a new flat before September.`,answers:[[`to find`]]},{id:`b1-vc-177`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use the present continuous for fixed arrangements with a time. Example: I'm seeing the doctor at three.`,wrongReasons:{meet:`The present simple does not show a personal arrangement; use 'I'm meeting'.`,"will meet":`This is an arrangement already made, so use the present continuous.`},text:`I'm busy on Monday. I ___ (meet) my bank manager at ten.`,answers:[[`am meeting`,`'m meeting`,`am going to meet`,`'m going to meet`]]},{id:`b1-vc-178`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`The past negative of 'be able to' is wasn't/weren't able to + base form. Example: She wasn't able to finish the race.`,wrongReasons:{"didn't be able to sleep":`Make the negative with 'wasn't', not 'didn't be'.`,"wasn't able sleep":`You need 'to' after 'able': able to sleep.`},text:`I ___ (not / be able to / sleep) last night because of the noise.`,answers:[[`wasn't able to sleep`,`was not able to sleep`]]},{id:`b1-vc-179`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'used to' + base form for repeated past actions. Example: I used to walk the dog every evening.`,wrongReasons:{"used to spent":`The verb after 'used to' stays in the base form: spend.`,"was used to spend":`Do not add 'was'; for a past habit write 'used to spend'.`},text:`When I was a student, I ___ (used to / spend) hours in the library.`,answers:[[`used to spend`]]},{id:`b1-vc-180`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Suggest' is followed by the -ing form (not 'to'). Example: She suggested going for a walk.`,wrongReasons:{"to meet":`'Suggest' is not followed by 'to' + verb; use -ing.`,meet:`After 'suggest', add -ing: meeting.`},text:`He suggested ___ (meet) at the café near the station.`,answers:[[`meeting`]]},{id:`b1-vc-181`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`future-forms`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'will' after 'Do you think...?' to ask for a prediction. Example: Do you think they will win?`,wrongReasons:{snows:`This asks about the future, so use 'will snow'.`,snowing:`You need 'will' before the verb, and the base form: will snow.`},text:`Do you think it ___ (snow) at Christmas this year?`,answers:[[`will snow`,`'ll snow`,`is going to snow`]]},{id:`b1-vc-182`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'should' + base form to ask for or give advice. Example: Should I bring a present?`,wrongReasons:{"should to book":`After 'should', use the base form without 'to'.`,"should booked":`Use the base form after 'should', not the past.`},text:`Do you think we ___ (should / book) a table, or is the restaurant usually empty?`,answers:[[`should book`]]},{id:`b1-vc-183`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`used-to`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`'Used to' + base form can describe past beliefs that are no longer true. Example: People used to think the Earth was flat.`,wrongReasons:{"used to thought":`After 'used to', write the base form 'think'.`,"use to think":`The positive form is 'used to', with -d.`},text:`Scientists ___ (used to / think) that the Earth was the centre of the universe.`,answers:[[`used to think`]]},{id:`b1-vc-184`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`gerund`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`After 'without', use the -ing form. Example: He left without paying.`,wrongReasons:{"to say":`'Without' is a preposition, so use -ing, not 'to'.`,say:`The verb after 'without' needs -ing: saying.`},text:`Lucy left the party early without ___ (say) goodbye.`,answers:[[`saying`]]},{id:`b1-vc-185`,type:`verb-conjugate`,level:`B1`,topic:`modals`,prompt:`Write the correct form of the verb in brackets.`,explanation:`Use 'may not be able to' + base form when something will possibly not be possible. Example: I may not be able to come tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{"may not can call":`Two modals cannot go together; use 'may not be able to'.`,"may not able to call":`You need 'be' before 'able to'.`},text:`My phone battery is almost dead, so I ___ (may / not / be able to / call) you later.`,answers:[[`may not be able to call`]]}]};export{e as default};